---
import Guide from "../../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import Code from "../../../components/Code.astro"
import DocLink from "../../../components/DocLink.astro"
import ScriptTable from "../../../components/ScriptTable.astro"
import CssFiles from "./partials/CssFiles.astro"
import InstallCommand from "./partials/InstallCommand.astro"
import NextSteps from "./partials/NextSteps.astro"
import PickAndChoose from "./partials/PickAndChoose.astro"
import QuickStart from "./partials/QuickStart.astro"

export const keywords = ["eleventy", "install", "macro", "npm", "setup", "ssr"]
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Getting started</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    OPUI ships every component as a Nunjucks 3 macro. Install the package, add
    the plugin, and import the macros you need.
  </Fragment>

  <section id="quick-start">
    <h2>Quick start</h2>
    <QuickStart>
      <Fragment slot="install">
        <InstallCommand packages="@11ty/eleventy nunjucks" />
        <Code
          code={`// eleventy.config.mjs
import opui from "opui-css/nunjucks/eleventy"

export default (eleventyConfig) => {
  eleventyConfig.addPlugin(opui)
  eleventyConfig.addPassthroughCopy({
    "node_modules/opui-css/dist/opui.css": "opui.css",
  })
}`}
          lang="js"
        />
        <Code
          code={`<!-- _includes/layout.njk -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/opui.css" />`}
          lang="html"
        />
      </Fragment>
      <Code
        slot="use"
        code={`{% from "opui/index.njk" import Button %}

{% call Button({ color: "primary", variant: "filled" }) %}Save{% endcall %}`}
        lang="html"
      />
    </QuickStart>
  </section>

  <section id="whats-in-the-css">
    <h2>What's in the CSS</h2>
    <CssFiles />
  </section>

  <section id="css">
    <h2>Link only what you use</h2>
    <p>
      Copy the CSS folders and your own <code>css</code> folder to your site:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`// eleventy.config.mjs
eleventyConfig.addPassthroughCopy({
  "node_modules/opui-css/core": "opui/core",
  "node_modules/opui-css/css": "opui/css",
})
eleventyConfig.addPassthroughCopy("css")`}
      lang="js"
    />
    <p>
      List the files in a CSS file, and link it instead of <code>opui.css</code
      >:
    </p>
    <PickAndChoose file="css/main.css" from="/opui" />
  </section>

  <section id="environment">
    <h2>Without Eleventy</h2>
    <p>Pass your environment to <code>configure</code>:</p>
    <Code
      code={`import nunjucks from "nunjucks"
import { configure } from "opui-css/nunjucks"

const env = configure(nunjucks.configure("views"))`}
      lang="js"
    />
    <p>
      It adds the macros under <code>opui/</code>, their filters and the
      <code>opui</code> global. It needs <code>autoescape</code> on and
      <code>trimBlocks</code>, <code>lstripBlocks</code> and
      <code>throwOnUndefined</code> off, the Nunjucks defaults. Your own
      templates come first, so <code>views/opui/Button.njk</code> replaces the
      Button macro. A replacement defines that one macro and imports any other
      from <code>opui/</code>: when two macros sit in one template, Nunjucks
      runs a <code>{"{% call %}"}</code> body with the variables of the macro it
      calls.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="usage">
    <h2>How to use</h2>
    <p>
      Each macro takes one dict with the component's props. The default slot is
      the body of <code>{"{% call %}"}</code>, and named slots go in
      <code>slots</code> under their camelCase name, as a string or captured
      with <code>{"{% set %}"}</code>:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`{% from "opui/index.njk" import Field %}

{% set start %}<b>@</b>{% endset %}
{% call Field({ for: "handle", label: "Handle", slots: { start: start } }) %}
  <input aria-describedby="handle-start" id="handle" />
{% endcall %}`}
      lang="html"
    />
    <p>
      Props are text, as in the other frameworks: a value marked
      <code>| safe</code> prints escaped, in attributes too, so markup goes in a
      slot. A prop set to <code>none</code> renders as a prop not given, so its
      default applies.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keys the macro doesn't take go to its element as attributes. Import a
      macro on its own with
      <code>{'{% from "opui/Button.njk" import Button %}'}</code>, or all of
      them as <code>{'{% import "opui/index.njk" as ui %}'}</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <DocLink href="/components/field">Field</DocLink> wraps your own
      control. Give the control an <code>id</code>, set <code>for</code> on the
      field to it, and name it like any input. For a hint, set the control's
      <code>aria-describedby</code> to <code>&lt;id&gt;-end-text</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="scripts">
    <h2>Scripts</h2>
    <p>The macros render markup only. A few features need a script:</p>
    <ScriptTable />
    <p>Without a bundler, copy the scripts to your site:</p>
    <Code
      code={`// eleventy.config.mjs
eleventyConfig.addPassthroughCopy({
  "node_modules/opui-css/css/js": "opui/css/js",
  "node_modules/opui-css/elements": "opui/elements",
})`}
      lang="js"
    />
    <p>And map the package in the <code>head</code>:</p>
    <Code
      code={`<script type="importmap">
  { "imports": { "opui-css/": "/opui/" } }
</script>`}
      lang="html"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="ids">
    <h2>Ids</h2>
    <p>
      Components a trigger points at need an <code>id</code>. Generated ids come
      from one counter per environment, so they never repeat, but their numbers
      follow the render order. Pass <code>id</code> where it has to stay the
      same, or your own generator as <code>configure(env, {"{ id }"})</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="warnings">
    <h2>Warnings</h2>
    <p>
      Unless <code>NODE_ENV</code> is <code>production</code>, a key that is no
      prop and no attribute of the element, or a slot the macro doesn't have,
      warns once:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`[OPUI Button] Unknown prop "lable": Button has no prop by that name and its element no attribute, so it renders as an attribute.`}
      lang="text"
    />
    <p>
      A macro called with anything but one dict, such as
      <code>{'Button("Save")'}</code>, throws there instead.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="next-steps">
    <h2>Next steps</h2>
    <NextSteps />
  </section>
</Guide>
